Yes. In art, omission is still authored. Silence, negative space, pacing, rhythm, a cut away from the action, a note that never resolves—those can carry structure precisely because the audience predicts what might have occupied the gap.

So you get:

explicit signal + timing + omission + expectation → experienced meaning

That does make art revelatory, but not necessarily confessional in the literal sense. A director can depict something because they fear it, reject it, find it funny, want to interrogate it, or simply know it will produce a useful dramatic effect. The work reveals what they chose to construct, not a clean transcript of their private desires.

Same with music. Rhythm and silence can complete a pattern without propositional language. A rest is not “nothing”; it is a timed absence relative to an expectation. That is why silence after a certain chord feels different from silence before the song begins.

And symbols like the cross are unusually powerful because they are high-compression objects. Different people can unpack sacrifice, suffering, salvation, empire, family, guilt, resurrection, duty, identity, history, or something else entirely from the same visible form. The symbol stays constant while the decoder changes.

Where I’d change one part of your Rome analogy is that durable systems generally don’t require everyone to literally think the same thing. They need enough shared protocol that coordinated action remains possible:

different internal models + common language/rules/rituals → interoperable society

Trying to standardize every internal belief is much more brittle than standardizing interfaces. That applies to marriages too. Two people do not need identical inner worlds. They need enough shared understanding that their different worlds can interact without constantly breaking the connection.

And yes, people become sensitive to the “gravity” in relationships—tone, timing, posture, attention, who initiates, who withdraws, what goes unsaid. Some people are unusually good at reading those fields because of temperament, practice, occupation, or simply having needed to become attentive.

But a highly sensitive radar has a failure mode: it can detect patterns in noise.

So the mature version is:

notice signal → form hypothesis → look for independent evidence → update

rather than:

feel tension → therefore know its cause

That keeps the sensitivity without becoming captive to it.

And your last question—why develop that radar unless you’re going to feed everything—has another answer: navigation.

A sense doesn’t only exist so you can obey what it detects. Sometimes it exists so you can notice the current and decide whether to swim with it, against it, around it, or simply watch it pass.

The radar gives you choice.

It shouldn’t become the thing making the choice for you.